Pre-note review criteria (recorded before opening docs/design/std-1.md)

Sources: docs/spec.md, especially 2.4, 4.1–4.8, 6.2/6.10, 9.1–9.5, 11.4/11.6/13; syntax.md replacement rules; relevant type/record and control/concurrency design decisions. The spec remains normative; proposed changes in the other design notes must not silently become existing language rules.

1. All four modules must be ordinary dependency-package i, with no privileged names, hidden casts, new primitives disguised as library definitions, or runtime equality overrides. New general language rules require independent set-of-values meaning, three unrelated uses, soundness and principal inference.
2. Streams: distinguish construction from forcing, preserve left-to-right ordering, report callback/source errors, compose service requirements without permitting unequal forms of one service, use services at force time, define retry/replay and early termination. Hard cases: force twice under different services; map/filter/flat_map with distinct errors and service types; take(0), zip with an empty side, errors after a prefix; infinite rejected filter prefix; a stream escaping a resource scope or holding a one-shot continuation; self-recursive streams and types; arbitrary user-made producer callbacks.
3. Sequences: persistent invariants and explicit costs in strict i, with constructible/checkable representations. Hard cases: nested finger tree recursion Tree(Node(A)) under monomorphic recursive bindings; empty/singleton, negative or huge indices, splitting at ends; map changing element type and shape; branching persistent reuse invalidating amortized costs; equality/hash of distinct representations; opaque type exports and all helper types in exported inferred schemes; internal invariant failure must not leak Assert unexpectedly.
4. Paths: specify lexical-only POSIX semantics independently of host, plus normalize/render/join/relative laws. Hard cases: empty and '.', '..' at relative and absolute roots, repeated and exactly double-leading '/', trailing separators, absolute RHS, hidden-file extensions, NUL and Unicode, no inference of filesystem containment through symlinks.
5. URIs: coherent RFC 3986 syntax, lossless presence distinctions and raw component handling; render(parse(s)) law; validated construction; exact normalization and resolution algorithms. Hard cases: absent vs empty authority/query/fragment/port; relative first-segment colon, leading //, authority plus rootless path; percent-encoded delimiters/dots/non-UTF8 bytes; IPv4 ambiguity, IPv6 and IPvFuture; malformed escapes; base with/without authority and empty path; normalization changing URI-reference grammar or meaning; query duplicate keys, '+', empty fields and separators.
6. Every requested 'clarification' must be compared with the normative wording: follows already, fills an actual gap, or changes accepted programs/meaning. Check citations against actual code; a compiler limitation is not itself a spec restriction. Require concrete positive/negative probes for recursion, variance, environment propagation, generalization, export opacity and inferred signatures where disputed.
7. A simpler rule wins when it meets the same semantic and cost requirements; don't report preference as a defect. Each finding needs a concrete witness, evidence status, source, and what could overturn it.
